From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Do not gate the back gesture on platform detection

The keyup -> `backgesture` emulation sits inside the block guarded by

    if (webOS.platform.legacy || webOS.platform.open || webOS.platform.luneos)

so on any build where those three are unset the listener is never installed and
an application has no back gesture at all. Patch 1 makes the detection work
again, but the gate is the wrong shape regardless: this library supersedes
webos-lib for Enyo 2.7, and webos-lib's source/BackGesture.js never gated it -

    enyo.dispatcher.listen(document, 'keyup', function(ev) {
        if (ev.keyCode == 27 || ev.keyIdentifier == "U+1200001"
                || ev.keyIdentifier == "U+001B" || ev.keyIdentifier == "Back") {
            enyo.Signals.send('onbackbutton', ev);
        }
    });

Introducing the guard is therefore a regression against the library this one
replaced, and it makes the back gesture depend on platform sniffing that can
only ever become more fragile. A listener that reacts to Escape and the two
webOS Back key identifiers costs nothing on a platform that never sends them.

So move it out, next to the other unconditional blocks, and keep the Mojo hooks
(relaunch, stageActivated/stageDeactivated, show/hide) behind the guard where
they belong - those really are legacy-container specific.

This also makes the gesture independent of how the container identifies itself,
which matters because the key is not a webOS-only convention: the LuneOS
compositor's CardView.onSwipeLeftGesture sends it as a plain key to whatever has
focus -

    seat.sendKeyEvent(Qt.Key_Escape, true);
    seat.sendKeyEvent(Qt.Key_Escape, false);

Verified on a MindPhone with this patch alone - patch 1's fallback and the
application-side workaround both compiled out, so webOS.platform stayed empty
and platform.luneos undefined: a keyup of keyCode 27 still raises `backgesture`,
and in Messaging a conversation open at composer left=87 in a 480px viewport
slides to left=567, thread list showing. Either patch fixes the back gesture on
its own; patch 1 additionally restores everything else that platform detection
gates.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending [not yet submitted to JayCanuck/enyo-luneos]
---
--- a/LuneOS/LuneOS.js
+++ b/LuneOS/LuneOS.js
@@ -59,6 +59,36 @@
 
 
 /* ============================================================================
+	Back gesture
+============================================================================*/
+(function() {
+
+	// Deliberately NOT inside the platform guard that the rest of the Mojo hooks live behind.
+	// webos-lib's BackGesture.js - what Enyo 2.5 and earlier apps used, and what this library
+	// supersedes for 2.7 - listened unconditionally:
+	//
+	//     enyo.dispatcher.listen(document, 'keyup', function(ev) {
+	//         if (ev.keyCode == 27 || ...) { enyo.Signals.send('onbackbutton', ev); }
+	//     });
+	//
+	// Gating it on webOS.platform.{legacy,open,luneos} is what broke the back gesture on current
+	// LuneOS, where all three are unset and the listener was therefore never installed. A keyup
+	// listener that only reacts to Escape and the webOS Back keys costs nothing anywhere, and the
+	// compositor's back gesture is exactly that key: CardView.onSwipeLeftGesture does
+	// seat.sendKeyEvent(Qt.Key_Escape, true/false) to the focused surface.
+	document.addEventListener('keyup', function(ev) {
+		if (ev.keyCode == 27 || ev.keyIdentifier == "U+1200001"
+				|| ev.keyIdentifier == "U+001B" || ev.keyIdentifier == "Back") {
+			var e = document.createEvent("Event");
+			e.initEvent("backgesture", false, false);
+			document.dispatchEvent(e);
+		}
+	});
+
+})();
+
+
+/* ============================================================================
 	Device Orientation APIs
 ============================================================================*/
